<description>
Expand the lower seed pixel into concentric square rings using the top-row code as the distance palette.

Input structure
The grid has a dominant background, one full-width separator row, a code row
immediately above that separator, and exactly one non-background seed pixel
below it whose color is the first code entry.
Rule
Read the code row from the left edge through its last non-background entry,
preserving any background entries inside that span as blank ring colors.  A
cell at Chebyshev distance d from the seed takes code[d], clipped to the
grid.  If a non-background code pixel lies directly above the seed column,
replace that consumed code pixel with the separator color.
Key insight
The top row is a one-dimensional palette indexed by square-ring radius; the
lower singleton only supplies the center of that expansion.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_in_order",
  "src": "top-row code cells s ordered left-to-right through the last non-background entry, including background cells as blank entries",
  "dst": "square rings d centered on the lower seed pixel, ordered inside-out by Chebyshev distance",
  "match_cond": "rank(s) == d.chebyshev_radius",
  "transform": "fill each ring d with s.color, leaving background-code rings blank; preserve the header and separator except a non-background code pixel directly above the seed column becomes the separator color",
  "symbol_instructions": "the top row is the radius-indexed palette; the lower singleton gives the center and must match the first palette color"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 16x16 [observed], bg=0 [observed]
  header:
    code_row: row 0, contains 4-6 palette entries starting at col 0
    separator_row: row 1, filled entirely with separator color (5 [observed])
    palette: left-aligned sequence of colors indexed by Chebyshev distance; code[0] is non-bg; may contain bg entries (=blank rings); code entries need not be distinct; no entry equals separator color
  seed:
    single non-bg pixel below separator; color == code[0]
    placement: row 6-11, col 5-10 [observed]; must satisfy seed_r - radius > separator_row (rings don't overlap header)
    radius: len(code)-1, so 3-5; rings may clip at grid edges (bottom/sides)
  output:
    concentric square rings centered on seed; ring at Chebyshev distance d gets color code[d]; bg-coded rings become bg (blank)
    if code[seed_col] is non-bg, that code pixel is replaced by separator color
    everything else unchanged

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick grid 16x16, bg=0, separator color=5 at row 1.
    2. Choose code length L in 4-6. Pick L palette colors (non-separator, code[0] != bg; others may be bg or repeated).
    3. Place code at row 0, cols 0..L-1; fill rest of row 0 with bg.
    4. Set radius = L-1. Pick seed position (sr, sc) in the lower region such that sr - radius > 1 (no header overlap). Also ensure the rings stay interesting (allow clipping at grid bottom/sides).
    5. Build output: stamp concentric Chebyshev rings centered at (sr, sc) with palette colors. If code[sc] is non-bg (sc < L), set output code_row[sc] = separator_color.
    6. Build input from output: erase all ring pixels (set to bg), restore code_row to original palette, place seed pixel at (sr, sc) with code[0].
```
